## 2. Logical Architecture & Request-Response Lifecycle

The lifecycle of an operation (such as adding an entity, generating a schedule, or manually moving a session) flows through an end-to-end synchronized pipeline:

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    actor User as User / Academic Admin
    participant UI as Frontend SPA (app.js)
    participant Server as Winsock Server (main.cpp)
    participant Router as REST Dispatcher (api.h)
    participant Engine as Scheduler & DSA (scheduler.h / dsa.h)
    participant Storage as File Storage (storage.h)

    User->>UI: Click "Generate Timetable"
    UI->>Server: POST /api/generate {"divisions": [0, 1]}
    Server->>Router: Parse HTTP headers & extract JSON payload
    Router->>Engine: generateTimetable(selectedDivs)

    activate Engine
    Note over Engine: 1. Clone active grid into tempGrid<br/>2. Re-compute faculty hours for unselected divs<br/>3. Place fixed breaks<br/>4. Populate MaxHeap with weighted sessions<br/>5. Pop sessions & test 7 constraints across days/slots<br/>6. Allocate dynamic flexible breaks in windows<br/>7. Commit tempGrid -> master grid
    Engine->>Engine: buildFacultyGraph() (Recalculate BFS dependencies)
    Engine-->>Router: Generation complete (0 unscheduled)
    deactivate Engine

    Router->>Storage: saveTimetables()
    Storage-->>Router: Persisted to data/timetables.txt
    Router-->>Server: HTTP 200 OK + JSON {status: "success", unscheduled: []}
    Server-->>UI: HTTP Response Payload
    UI->>UI: Re-render Timetable Grid & Analytics Cards
    UI-->>User: Visual Update & Success Toast
```

